<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <MSBuildAllProjects>$(MSBuildAllProjects);$(MSBuildThisFileFullPath)</MSBuildAllProjects>
    <HasSharedItems>true</HasSharedItems>
    <SharedGUID>f5a6b7c8-d9e0-4f1a-b2c3-4d5e6f7a8b9c</SharedGUID>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <Import_RootNamespace>Birko.Health.Redis</Import_RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!-- External dependency: StackExchange.Redis — CONSUMER-SUPPLIED for this project alone:

         <PackageReference Include="StackExchange.Redis" Version="2.*" />

       Every other Birko project that wraps a driver declares it, so importing the project is all a
       consumer has to do. This one is the documented exception, and both alternatives were measured
       before choosing it (TASK-234):

       It cannot declare the driver, because Birko.Redis.projitems does and Birko.Sandbox imports
       both — two PackageReference items in one project file is NU1504, an error under the
       -warnaserror the build sweep runs.

       It also must not depend on Birko.Redis to inherit that declaration. RedisSettings extends
       RemoteSettings and implements ILoadable, so importing it drags Configuration, Data.Core,
       Data.Stores, Contracts and Time behind it — five shared projects, into a consumer that wanted
       one health check. A consumer takes only the subset it needs, and that would break the rule.

       In practice this costs nothing: RedisHealthCheck takes an IConnectionMultiplexer the consumer
       already has, and a consumer importing Birko.Redis as well gets the driver from there. Declare
       the line above only if you import this project on its own. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)RedisHealthCheck.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
